{"solutions":[{"index":0,"answer":"In a RAW processor (linear data), the sequence that maximizes recovery is: (1) Pull Highlights/Whites down first — RAW linear data retains more highlight information than the preview shows, so recovering highlights before any global adjustment prevents irreversible clipping. (2) Lift Shadows/Blacks — recovers foreground rock detail; doing this second avoids pushing highlights further. (3) Set overall Exposure — adjust the midpoint only after extremes are recovered so neither end re-clips. (4) Apply tone curve / contrast — adds punch without destroying recovered range. (5) Apply noise reduction — shadow lifting amplifies sensor noise, so NR must follow shadow recovery. (6) Sharpen last — sharpening amplifies noise and edge artifacts, so it follows NR. This order works because RAW editing is non-destructive and operates on linear sensor data; recovering clipped extremes before global exposure or contrast moves prevents permanent data loss, and deferring NR/sharpening to the end avoids amplifying artifacts introduced by earlier tonal recovery steps.","limitations":"","assumptionsRequired":[]}]}
